\subsection{Sampling}

The sampling step consists of generating permutations from the model obtained in the learning stage. The sampling algorithm depends on the distance considered in the model. 

%
The method for sampling the GM model with the Kendall's-$\tau$ distance is called:
\begin{verbatim}
[pop] = GMallows_kendall_sampling(n,model,Card,AuxPop,AuxFunVal,sampling_params)
\end{verbatim}
\noindent
where $n$ is the number of variables (size) of the permutation, \textit{model} is the model learned by the learning method, \textit{Card} is a matrix with the dimensions of all the variables, \textit{AuxPop} is the population from which the model was learned, and \textit{AuxFunVal} is the evaluation (fitness values) of the \textit{AuxPop} data set. The \textit{sampling\_params} are the additional sampling parameters (we follow the general scheme of the Mateda-2.0 toolbox). In this case, our methods only require one parameter, \textit{N}, which is the number of new individuals to be generated.


